+### 0210 — A derived call graph that only sees `f(x)` covers 3 of 7 guards and looks complete
+
+- ROOT CAUSE: `utf8_subsumption_test.go` pinned "no exported entry point hands an already-decoded value to a `utf8.ValidString` re-check" over an AST call graph that recorded an edge only for `call.Fun.(*ast.Ident)` naming a top-level declaration, and skipped `Recv != nil` in both derivations. This package dispatches through a function-value table — validators registered in `mustBuildImmutableObjectShapeValidators` and invoked as `validator(object)` in `validateImmutableObjectShape` — so neither the registration nor the invocation was an edge.
+- EVIDENCE: review mutant H, an exported `ValidateDecodedRecord(map[string]any)` reaching the guards through that table, left all three pins green and the whole package suite `ok`. Mutant I, the same body as an exported method, survived for the second reason. The producer's own passing mutant D2 called a guard by direct identifier — the single shape the graph could see. Measured coverage was 3/7 guarded functions; `validateSessionLaunchPlan`, `validateSessionBoardIdentity`, `validateSortedUniqueStrings` and `validateProviderIdentityRecord` were pinned vacuously.
+- FINDING: an AST call graph is a claim about a *modelled subset* of dispatch, and its blind spot is invisible from inside — every test still passes, and the structure looks more thorough the longer it gets. A reachability pin must publish its coverage as a ratio; prose about what it checks cannot distinguish 7/7 from 3/7.
+- DECISION: the graph now records edges for calls through a function value — a callee that is not a package function, a predeclared identifier, or a named type is treated as reaching every function whose identifier is used as a value in the package — and includes methods in both derivations. Coverage is now 7/7 and is asserted, not narrated: `TestEveryUTF8RecheckIsCoveredByTheEntryPointPin` fails and names the uncovered functions if any guard drops out of reach.
+- DECISION: the published claim was narrowed in the same change. `closed_shapes.go` no longer says the invariant "cannot be broken silently" and `README.md` no longer says a map-taking entry point simply "reddens the pin". Both now state the modelled constructions and name what still evades them: reflection, a function value handed to another package, and a func-typed struct field, since `x.M(...)` resolves only when `M` is a method declared in this package. Fixing the graph without narrowing the claim moves a false guarantee one construction outward rather than removing it.
+- EVIDENCE: mutants H, I and I2 (byte-parameter method — the receiver is still caller-supplied decoded state) all redden `TestNoExportedEntryPointHandsADecodedValueToAUTF8Recheck`, each naming all 7 guarded functions. Narrowing mutant K removes only the function-value edge: the clean tree stays green and mutant H survives, which is what identifies that edge as the decisive addition rather than something else in the rewrite. Mutant K2 removes the computed-callee edge as well and reproduces the review's measurement exactly — `coverage is 3/7`, naming the same four functions.
+- FINDING: a guard that no mutant can redden is not covered, however thorough the derivation around it looks. The ratio is the honest report; "machine-checked" is not.
